From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 16:57:47 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: precompile bounded raster executable classes

A pipeline compiles a bounded set of raster executable classes
(kk_raster_variant.h) instead of one raster shader per combination of
dynamic state. BASE keeps fills and eligible native lines on Metal's direct
path. POINT draws polygon-mode POINT as oriented squares that keep the
source polygon's facingness, so polygon-point emulation no longer needs its
own indirect setup dispatch. LINE, RECTANGULAR and SMOOTH materialize the
three line modes through a compute scan that clips each source polygon once
and publishes one 64-byte record per clipped edge (kk_raster_edge.h), never
a record per pixel. DISCARD keeps the application stages and transform
feedback without rasterizing. Provoking vertex, stipple enable and depth
clipping stay dynamic within a class; only a change that needs another
raster shader needs another class.

kk_raster_variant_mask works out from the pipeline's static and dynamic
state which classes a draw could ever select, and only those are compiled,
once, at complete pipeline creation or at a library's final link or
vertex-input resolution. At draw time kk_cmd_select_line_raster_variant
applies kk_raster_variant_select to the current dynamic state and binds the
selected class's stages. Shader positions never become unchecked counts or
addresses: the scan bounds viewport coordinates, and an instance that would
overflow suppresses the whole draw. kosmickrisp_raster_variant checks the
selection against the masks for every primitive and dynamic state, and
kosmickrisp_raster_discard covers the DISCARD class with transform feedback.

The line-planner test helper also had to survive release builds.
kk_test_line_planner_bounds hands Metal a commit feedback block that points
at a stack-allocated completion struct, and the only thing that kept that
block from outliving the frame was a dispatch_semaphore_wait inside
assert(). Every build that defines NDEBUG - Mesa's own release buildtype,
which is what dependencies/build-apple-gpu.sh configures - dropped the wait
with the rest of the assertion, so the helper committed the dispatch,
released the semaphore and returned while Metal still held the block. The
block then ran on com.Metal4.CompletionQueue and signalled a semaphore that
no longer existed, reading through a stale stack pointer: SIGSEGV in
dispatch_semaphore_signal from
__mtl_commit_options_add_feedback_handler_block_invoke, three times out of
three, with and without the Metal validation layer. A debugoptimized build
never saw it, because there the wait is real.

Check with a macro that survives NDEBUG instead.  The wait is not the only
assertion that mattered: under NDEBUG the whole helper verified nothing at all
while still running the dispatch - the buffer guard bytes, the planner's
indirect draw arguments and the edge bounds it exists to check all compiled
away - so every check in the file becomes KK_TEST_CHECK.  A timeout is now
fatal too, because falling through one would leave Metal holding the same
dangling block.

